/P 370 0 R
/T (Part-2 This is a really serious and complex question: why is the crime rate among teenagers seemingly increasing? There isn't one simple answer, but I think several factors are contributing.)
/E (Part-2 This is a really serious and complex question: why is the crime rate among teenagers seemingly increasing? There isn't one simple answer, but I think several factors are contributing.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 370 0 R
/T (In short, it's a multifaceted problem that needs a comprehensive approach, focusing on support, education, and opportunity.)
/E (In short, it's a multifaceted problem that needs a comprehensive approach, focusing on support, education, and opportunity.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 370 0 R
/T (One major reason is the influence of social media and technology. Many young people are exposed to violent content online, which can normalize aggressive behavior. For example, some studies suggest that teenagers who play violent video games may become desensitized to violence and more likely to act out in real life. Furthermore, socio-economic factors play a big role. Poverty, unstable home environments, and lack of parental supervision can create a breeding ground for crime. When families are struggling, or when children don't have consistent guidance, they are more vulnerable. For instance, a teenager from a disadvantaged background might resort to theft out of desperation or a lack of other options. Finally, some argue that mental health issues are also a contributing factor. Untreated depression, anxiety, or other challenges can manifest in destructive ways, including anti-social behaviour.)
/E (One major reason is the influence of social media and technology. Many young people are exposed to violent content online, which can normalize aggressive behavior. For example, some studies suggest that teenagers who play violent video games may become desensitized to violence and more likely to act out in real life. Furthermore, socio-economic factors play a big role. Poverty, unstable home environments, and lack of parental supervision can create a breeding ground for crime. When families are struggling, or when children don't have consistent guidance, they are more vulnerable. For instance, a teenager from a disadvantaged background might resort to theft out of desperation or a lack of other options. Finally, some argue that mental health issues are also a contributing factor. Untreated depression, anxiety, or other challenges can manifest in destructive ways, including anti-social behaviour.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
